# TEMPLATE — copy to challenges.toml (gitignored) and fill in the blank secrets:
#   cp challenges.toml.example challenges.toml
# challenges.toml is the SINGLE source of truth for flags + the two forensics
# secrets (admin_pass, secret_host). The hub reads it for flag-checking; bring-up
# recipes + build scripts read it via scripts/secret.py. Get the real values from
# the private solutions repo (les-simple-ctf-sols).
#
# Challenge manifest — this is the one file instructors edit to add a tile.
#
# Each [[challenge]] becomes a tile on the hub. Fields:
#   id          unique slug (used in URLs and to record solves) — never reuse
#   title       shown on the tile and challenge page
#   category    small label on the tile, e.g. "Web / SQLi", "Network / Sniffing"
#   order       sort order on the grid (ascending)
#   link        (optional) URL of the running vuln service; shown as a button.
#               leave "" for challenges with no single web endpoint (e.g. LAN sniffing)
#   description markdown; task setup + how to connect. Do NOT leak the flag here.
#   flag        the static flag string students must submit
#
# After editing, `just up` picks it up (the file is bind-mounted; no rebuild).

[[challenge]]
id          = "warmup"
title       = "Warm-up: First Flag"
category    = "Intro"
order       = 1
link        = ""
description = """
Welcome to the arena. This tile exists so you can learn the flow before the real
targets bite back.

Flags always look like `CTF{...}`. This one is sitting right here in the briefing:

```
CTF{welcome_to_the_arena}
```

Paste it into the box below and hit **Submit flag**. Your tile turns green and the
progress counter ticks up. That's the whole game — except every other flag is hidden
inside a service you have to break.
"""
flag        = "CTF{welcome_to_the_arena}"   # warmup flag is public by design (shown above)

[[challenge]]
id          = "sniff-secretstore"
title       = "Whispers on the Wire"
category    = "Network / Sniffing"
order       = 2
link        = "http://secretstore.local"
source      = "sniff-http-secretstore"
admin_pass  = ""     # sniffable creds (not a flag); consumed by scripts/secret.py
description = """
Someone left a packet capture lying around: [**challenge.pcap**](/static/files/challenge.pcap).

It's a slice of ordinary network traffic — browsing, API calls, the usual chatter. But
somewhere in there, an admin signed in to the **Secret Store** (`http://secretstore.local`)
over plain **HTTP**. HTTP sends everything in the clear.

Open the capture in **Wireshark**, dig through the noise, and find what the admin typed.
Then do the obvious thing with it: the flag is a secret sitting in *their* vault, and only
*they* can read it.

*Tip: not every login you see is the one you want.*
"""
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "hidden-vault"
title       = "The Hidden Vault"
category    = "Network / TLS"
order       = 3
link        = ""
source      = "sni-hidden-vault"
secret_host = ""   # hidden SNI (not a flag); consumed by scripts/secret.py
description = """
So the Secret Store switched to **HTTPS**. The login is encrypted now — you can't read the
traffic anymore. Here's a capture to prove it: [**hidden-vault.pcap**](/static/files/hidden-vault.pcap).

Go ahead, try to read it. It's all TLS — the contents are sealed.

But encryption hides *what* you send, not *where* you send it. Every HTTPS connection still
announces its destination host in the clear, before the crypto starts. Skim the capture:
most of these hosts are perfectly ordinary. **One of them shouldn't exist.**

Find that host and pay it a visit.
"""
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "eval-calculator"
title       = "The Calculator"
category    = "Web / Injection"
order       = 4
link        = "http://localhost:8081"
description = """
Someone shipped a handy little **scientific calculator** as a web service:
[open it](http://localhost:8081). Type an expression, get a result — `sin`, `cos`, `sqrt`,
`pi`, all there.

It's more powerful than it looks. The server hands your input straight to the language that
runs it, and that language has more in scope than just arithmetic. See what else is in the room.

*(Running locally? The service is at `http://localhost:8081`. On the classroom network, swap in
the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "eval-calculator"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "eval-calculator-hardened"
title       = "The Calculator, Hardened"
category    = "Web / Injection"
order       = 5
link        = "http://localhost:8082"
description = """
The calculator leaked, so the team [locked it down](http://localhost:8082): it now evaluates with
**only the math library in scope** — the secret's been pulled out. Nothing to dump. Case closed.

Except a locked room still has doors. The engine is the same, and it can still reach outside
itself — the secret just moved somewhere else on the box. Go get it.

*(Running locally? The service is at `http://localhost:8082`. On the classroom network, swap in
the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "eval-calculator-hardened"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "sqli-taut-num"
title       = "SecureNotes v1 — Note Viewer"
category    = "Web / SQLi"
order       = 6
link        = "http://localhost:8083"
description = """
An `eval` in a service is rare — but plenty of services forget that **SQL is also a language**.
[SecureNotes](http://localhost:8083) lets you pull up a note by its numeric **ID**, and it builds
that lookup by pasting your input straight into a query.

Some notes are marked unpublished. Convince the database to hand them over anyway. The ID is a
number — you won't even need a quote.

*(Local: `http://localhost:8083`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "sqli-taut-num"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "sqli-taut-str"
title       = "SecureNotes v2 — Staff Directory"
category    = "Web / SQLi"
order       = 7
link        = "http://localhost:8084"
description = """
Same bug, different spot. The [staff directory](http://localhost:8084) looks a colleague up by
**username** — and this time your input lands *inside a quoted string*. The `admin` account is
filtered out of normal results, and its (plaintext) password is the prize.

Break out of the quote.

*(Local: `http://localhost:8084`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "sqli-taut-str"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "sqli-union"
title       = "SecureNotes v3 — Knowledge Base"
category    = "Web / SQLi"
order       = 8
link        = "http://localhost:8085"
description = """
The [knowledge base search](http://localhost:8085) echoes back whatever rows it finds — a wide-open
channel if you can make it return rows from somewhere *else*. The flag isn't in the notes table.
It's in another one. Go and read it.

*(Local: `http://localhost:8085`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "sqli-union"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "sqli-blind"
title       = "SecureNotes v4 — Username Checker"
category    = "Web / SQLi"
order       = 9
link        = "http://localhost:8086"
description = """
No more free data: the [signup name checker](http://localhost:8086) only ever tells you *"taken"*
or *"available."* That's still one bit of truth per request — and one bit is all you need to read
a secret out, one character at a time. You'll want to script this one.

*(Local: `http://localhost:8086`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "sqli-blind"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "sqli-subquery"
title       = "SecureNotes v5 — Field Previewer"
category    = "Web / SQLi"
order       = 10
link        = "http://localhost:8087"
description = """
The [field previewer](http://localhost:8087) shows one column of the latest note — and your input
*is* the column it selects. They've blocked `UNION`, so the v3 move is out. But UNION was never the
only way to read another table.

*(Local: `http://localhost:8087`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "sqli-subquery"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "sqli-subquery-nested"
title       = "SecureNotes v6 — Shared With You"
category    = "Web / SQLi"
order       = 11
link        = "http://localhost:8088"
description = """
[This page](http://localhost:8088) lists the notes shared to a username — but your input is buried
*inside a subquery*, not the main query. Naively injecting just throws a syntax error. Figure out
what you need to close first, then reach the outer query.

*(Local: `http://localhost:8088`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "sqli-subquery-nested"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "sqli-sanitize"
title       = "SecureNotes v7 — Firewalled"
category    = "Web / SQLi"
order       = 12
link        = "http://localhost:8089"
description = """
The final version [added a firewall](http://localhost:8089): send a quote, a `UNION`, a `;` or a
comment and the request is rejected outright. The note viewer underneath is still injectable in its
numeric ID slot, and there's a hidden note to reach — you just have to do it **without typing any
of the forbidden characters**. SQL is more flexible about how you spell things than the filter
assumes.

*(Local: `http://localhost:8089`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "sqli-sanitize"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "xss-stored"
title       = "Feedback Box"
category    = "Web / Client-Side"
order       = 13
link        = "http://localhost:8092"
description = """
The [Feedback Box](http://localhost:8092) lets anyone leave a message — and an **admin reviews new
feedback every couple of minutes** in their own browser. Their review page trusts what you submit a
little too much.

Their session carries a secret. Make their browser hand it to you. Open the **Listener** tab to get
your private beacon address, then watch it for whatever their browser sends (or run your own `ncat`).

*(Local: `http://localhost:8092`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "xss-stored"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "xss-reflected"
title       = "KnowledgeBase Search"
category    = "Web / Client-Side"
order       = 14
link        = "http://localhost:8093"
description = """
The [KnowledgeBase search](http://localhost:8093) drops your query straight back into the results
page. You can also **report a link** for a moderator to review — and the moderator's browser can
reach a page yours can't.

The flag isn't a cookie this time; it's on a console only the moderator can open. Get their browser
to read it and send it your way. Grab your private beacon address from the **Listener** tab (or use
your own `ncat`).

*(Local: `http://localhost:8093`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "xss-reflected"
flag        = ""

[[challenge]]
id          = "csrf"
title       = "VaultKeep"
category    = "Web / Client-Side"
order       = 15
link        = "http://localhost:8094"
description = """
[VaultKeep](http://localhost:8094) keeps a private recovery note that **only the admin can publish**.
No injection here — the admin **opens links you report** every couple of minutes, and their browser
does what your page tells it to, using their session.

Craft a page that makes the admin publish the note without meaning to. The result shows up on
`/public`. (The challenge README has the exact host-a-page steps.)

*(Local: `http://localhost:8094`; on the classroom network, swap in the teacher host.)*
"""
source      = "csrf"
flag        = ""
